An audit sweep for unfinished work, and a look at the live database rather than
at the code that talks about it.

1. render.ts CLAIMED THE PHOTOGRAPHS WERE DELIBERATELY OFF. MINE.

A status block near the top said, at length, that the photographic path was
built, tested and "NOT YET WIRED TO THE INTERFACE. ON PURPOSE" — that measured
side by side "this does not yet win", that the drawn floor was the better
picture a visitor was owed, and that what remained was anisotropic sampling.

Every word of it was true when written and none of it is true now. /design and
/floor-studio both pass `grain`. A wrong comment in the most-read file in this
renderer is worse than no comment: the next person to open it would have
believed the photographs were off on purpose and left them off.

The diagnosis in it was also wrong, which is the more useful correction. It was
not one missing feature. It was six separate arithmetic defects — the pyramid
four levels too shallow, the level measured against the wrong origin, a
fraction read as a distance, grain running across the board in four tiles out
of five, square tiles of a thing four feet long, and a ratio built in linear
light and applied to gamma-encoded colour. Each one alone made a photograph of
real oak look worse than two hex colours, which is exactly why it sat unwired
rather than being noticed. All six are named in the replacement, with the
patch that fixed each and the measurement that showed it: 19.0 against the
drawn path's 2.9, now 3.62 against 3.64.

2. THE SHOP SELLS AT PLACEHOLDER PRICES, AND THE WARNING SAYING SO WAS ONLY A
   COMMENT.

seed-products.ts carried "⚠️ PRICES ARE PLACEHOLDER MATERIAL RATES — replace
before this goes live". It went live. Checked against the production database:
seventeen active Product rows, and every basePrice matches this file exactly.
/mypage renders them behind sign-in and /api/shop/checkout opens a Stripe
session from them. Prices are recomputed server-side so a customer cannot
tamper with them — they can only be charged the placeholder.

Nothing has been bought. All seven Order rows are PENDING, created on one day,
none with a stripePaymentIntentId: abandoned sessions, not sales. The three
COMPLETED payments in the database are against Invoice rows, which is the
estimator flow with prices a person set. So this is a loaded gun and not a
wound, and it has been one since July.

A comment is not a safety catch. The script now exits without touching the
database unless SHOP_PRICES_ARE_REAL=1 is set — checked before the first write,
so a refusal cannot leave it half-seeded — and prints the price ranges it was
about to write. Setting that variable is a person saying in one deliberate act
that these are the numbers Ecowoods charges.

The rows already in the database are NOT touched. Changing a live business's
prices is not a script's decision and it is not mine; prisma/README-SHOP-PRICES.md
records exactly what is in production and gives both exits — correct the prices
and re-run, or `update ecowoods."Product" set active = false` to close the path
until they exist. Doing neither is also a decision and it is the one currently
in force.

3. A PLACEHOLDER WARNING THAT OUTLIVED ITS PROBLEM.

home-client.tsx carried "⚠️ PLACEHOLDER FIGURES ... REPLACE THEM WITH REAL
NUMBERS before this ships" directly above the comment recording those figures
already being removed, and above an array that has only labels in it. A
standing warning about a fixed defect trains the next reader to skim past the
ones that are still real.

4. TWO COMMENTS POINTED AT A FILE THAT DOES NOT EXIST.

work-map.ts and BookingPanel.tsx both referred the reader to
ops/DOMINATION-RUNBOOK.md, which is not in this repository, for the one thing
they needed. The coordinate task is now written where the coordinates are: open
each neighbourhood on a mapping source, read the centroid to three places,
replace the pair, flip COORDS_VERIFIED. The JSON-LD is already written and
gated on that flag. COORDS_SOURCE's text no longer names the missing file
either; it is exported but never imported, so it was never rendered, but a
string describing an internal path has no business in a constant that could be.

Not changed, and why: RelatedContent.tsx and CountUp.tsx are genuinely orphaned
and were left alone — deleting a file another agent may be mid-way through is
worse than a dead one. @ecowoods/ui is unimported but is a declared dependency,
and removing it edits pnpm-lock.yaml, which I cannot regenerate here. The
booking panel's missing calendar integration and the unreviewed legal pages are
both real gaps that are deliberately disclosed where a visitor can see them.

65 of 66 guards here; verify:css needs an install. parse-scan clean on 643
files. All six renderer harnesses and both service suites pass.

